 "case:briefing.0": "Seahouses, Northumberland. Wednesday 1 October 2025. Three miles out to sea lies St Ebba’s Isle: a seabird reserve with a ruined chapel, a lighthouse, and two rangers who live out there from April to October. Day visitors cross on Dunn’s Boats’ Ebba Belle. Sunday was the last landing day of the season.",
 "case:briefing.1": "A rare bird, a Siberian rubythroat, brought forty people across on Sunday. That afternoon, at the island’s Landing, forty return tickets were scanned onto the last boat. At Seahouses, thirty-nine people got off. The missing passenger was Nell Ashby, 39, Head of Reserves at the Northumberland Seabird Trust. On Monday morning the lifeboat found her body in the sea below the island’s north end.",
 "case:briefing.2": "The police believe she went up to the open upper deck as the boat rounded the north end in a heavy swell, and fell overboard unseen. At 16:22 she had messaged her husband, Tom, to get on and save her a seat, and her ticket was scanned four minutes later. The Maritime and Coastguard Agency is investigating Dunn’s Boats. Rob Dunn, the skipper, has had his licence suspended, and online he’s ‘the man who lost a passenger and didn’t notice’.",
 "case:briefing.3": "Nell’s sister, Bea Lowther, has hired you. ‘Nell was sick on every boat she ever got on. She sat inside, by the door, every single time. She texted Tom to save her a seat inside. Inside! And they want me to believe she climbed a ladder to the top deck in a swell?’",
 "case:briefing.4": "The trust has given you a crossing on its work boat. The two rangers are closing the island for the winter and leave on Friday. Everyone who was on St Ebba’s on Sunday afternoon is still close by.",
 "case:epilogue.0": "Morag Heslop made a full statement at Alnwick police station on the Thursday, with a solicitor beside her and her hands folded on the table. She was charged with Nell’s murder that evening. The following week a police diver brought Nell’s phone up from four fathoms of water off the end of the jetty.",
 "case:epilogue.1": "She had chosen the Churn because nobody would hear them. When Nell said ‘twenty-four wonderful years’, she pushed her over the wall, and a minute later answered Rob Dunn on the radio with the Churn booming behind her. From Gaz’s cliff path, her bottle-green fleece was only ‘dark clothes’. She put Nell’s phone into airplane mode from its lock screen, pushed the binoculars behind the paint tins in the oil store she had been clearing all afternoon, and wrote ‘locked for the winter’ in the day book. At the Landing she let the phone back onto the Wi-Fi in her pocket for eighty-five seconds, read Tom’s messages on the lock screen, and answered him. She knew where Nell always sat: she had watched her by that cabin door for fifteen years. She didn’t know how Nell typed. After that it was arithmetic: a scanner counts tickets, not people.",
 "case:epilogue.2": "The MCA’s report said the same. Rob Dunn got his licence back in November. Kirsty had told the investigators the truth about her counter before anyone needed to ask her twice. Since the first sailing of 2026, Dunn’s Boats count every head on and every head off, and say the number out loud to each other.",
 "case:epilogue.3": "Tom told Bea about the money the day after the funeral, and Bea told him Nell had already forgiven him: she’d said so on the phone on Saturday night. Gaz Milburn pleaded guilty to disturbing seals, gave the police every frame he had shot that afternoon, and hasn’t posted a word about the trust since. In March the trust’s board passed Nell’s plan with one change: a resident ranger stays on St Ebba’s, and it’s Sam. The keepers’ cottage is the Nell Ashby Research Base now.",
 "case:epilogue.4": "In May, when the puffins came back, Bea and Tom took Nell out to the north end on the Ebba Belle. Kirsty counted them aboard. They didn’t go up to the top deck. They sat inside, by the cabin door, with the window open, and let her go into the wake.",
 "case:hints.h_accuse.levels.0": "Nell never left the island. Who was with her at the Churn, and then at the Landing with her phone?",
 "case:hints.h_accuse.levels.1": "Only one person was free at the north end just before four and at the Landing at 16:22.",
 "case:hints.h_accuse.levels.2": "The killer is Morag Heslop: she pushed Nell into the Churn before the boat left, then answered Tom from her phone, to stop a plan that would end the island’s rangers. Prove it with your answer about where Nell was last seen, your answer about the Churn and the Landing, and your answer about why Nell came.",
 "case:hints.h_accuse_ready.levels.0": "For each proof slot, ask: which card shows where and when Nell really died, which puts the killer at the Churn, and which shows what Nell came to tell?",
 "case:hints.h_accuse_ready.levels.1": "The chance: your answer about the count, the message or the Churn, or the timeline. At the Churn: your answer about who could have been at the Churn and at the Landing, Kirsty’s statement about the radio, Morag’s own admissions, or Nell’s binoculars. The motive: your answer about why Nell came, the trust’s review, or Sam’s statement about Friday’s email.",
 "case:hints.h_accuse_ready.levels.2": "Accuse Morag Heslop: she pushed Nell into the Churn before the boat left, then answered Tom from her phone, to stop a plan that would end the island’s rangers. Prove it with your answer about where Nell was last seen, your answer about the Churn and the Landing, and your answer about why Nell came.",
