 "case:locations.cottage.description": "The keepers’ cottage is two rooms and a porch, whitewashed, smelling of paraffin and drying waterproofs. The walls are a metre thick: inside, you can’t hear the sea at all. Boxes are half packed for the winter: Morag and Sam are closing the island and leave on Friday’s work boat. The trust’s laptop is on the kitchen table, and Sam doesn’t mind you looking round.",
 "case:locations.cottage.hotspots.hs_ct_book.label": "The day book",
 "case:locations.cottage.hotspots.hs_ct_book.text": "On the desk under the window lies the island’s day book, a ledger as thick as a family Bible.",
 "case:locations.cottage.hotspots.hs_ct_notice.label": "The noticeboard",
 "case:locations.cottage.hotspots.hs_ct_notice.text": "Tide tables, a puffin rota, a postcard of the Farnes and the season’s boat times, all curling in the damp.",
 "case:locations.cottage.hotspots.hs_ct_photos.label": "The photo wall",
 "case:locations.cottage.hotspots.hs_ct_photos.text": "Twenty-four summers of rangers and volunteers, squinting into the wind on the jetty. Morag is in every one of them, her hair a little greyer each year.",
 "case:locations.cottage.hotspots.hs_ct_radio.label": "The radio",
 "case:locations.cottage.hotspots.hs_ct_radio.text": "The base-station radio, with two handsets charging beside it. Rangers carry one each, all day. Everything said on channel 6 is heard in the cottage, and on the boat, and on the other handset.",
 "case:locations.cottage.name": "The Rangers’ Cottage",
 "case:locations.cottage.summary": "The old keepers’ cottage by the lighthouse: the day book, the radio and the noticeboard.",
 "case:locations.harbour.art.props.0.label": "Quay",
 "case:locations.harbour.art.props.1.label": "Dunn’s kiosk",
 "case:locations.harbour.art.props.2.label": "Ebba Belle",
 "case:locations.harbour.art.props.3.label": "Wheelhouse",
 "case:locations.harbour.art.props.4.label": "Cabin (upper deck above)",
 "case:locations.harbour.art.props.6.label": "Stern deck",
 "case:locations.harbour.art.props.7.label": "Ladder",
 "case:locations.harbour.description": "Seahouses harbour on a grey Wednesday: lobster pots stacked on the quay, a gull on every bollard. The Ebba Belle lies against the inner wall, tied up for the winter now that the season is over. Dunn’s ticket kiosk is shuttered, with a handwritten sign in the window: ‘SAILINGS SUSPENDED — SORRY’. Kirsty lets you aboard.",
 "case:locations.harbour.hotspots.hs_hb_cabin.label": "The bench by the cabin door",
 "case:locations.harbour.hotspots.hs_hb_cabin.text": "Inside the cabin, eight rows of benches face forward under streaming windows. The bench nearest the back door has the best of the fresh air and a view of the horizon: Nell’s seat, Bea says, on every crossing. On Sunday Tom sat here, with his coat on the seat beside him.",
 "case:locations.harbour.hotspots.hs_hb_drawer.label": "The wheelhouse drawer",
 "case:locations.harbour.hotspots.hs_hb_drawer.text": "Kirsty opens the drawer under the wheel and takes out the counter she uses at every boarding.",
 "case:locations.harbour.hotspots.hs_hb_kiosk.label": "Dunn’s kiosk",
 "case:locations.harbour.hotspots.hs_hb_kiosk.text": "A poster on the kiosk’s shutter: ‘TICKETS ON YOUR PHONE — show the code at the gangway. One phone can hold your whole party’s tickets!’",
 "case:locations.harbour.hotspots.hs_hb_ladder.label": "The ladder",
 "case:locations.harbour.hotspots.hs_hb_ladder.text": "An iron ladder up from the stern deck to the upper deck, almost vertical. You need both hands for it in a flat calm. In Sunday’s swell, Kirsty says, the birders went up it ‘like sailors in a storm’.",
 "case:locations.harbour.hotspots.hs_hb_upper.label": "The upper deck",
 "case:locations.harbour.hotspots.hs_hb_upper.text": "Kirsty takes you up the ladder to the open deck above the cabin.",
 "case:locations.harbour.hotspots.hs_hb_wheel.label": "The wheelhouse",
 "case:locations.harbour.hotspots.hs_hb_wheel.text": "Rob Dunn’s chair, the radio, a chart of the island with every rock pencilled in, and a photo of his father at this same wheel in 1964. From here the skipper can’t see the stern of the upper deck at all.",
 "case:locations.harbour.name": "Seahouses Harbour",
 "case:locations.harbour.summary": "The Ebba Belle at her winter mooring, and Dunn’s ticket kiosk on the quay.",
 "case:locations.landing.art.props.0.label": "The cove",
 "case:locations.landing.art.props.1.label": "Jetty",
 "case:locations.landing.art.props.2.label": "Visitor hut",
 "case:locations.landing.art.props.3.label": "Sightings board",
 "case:locations.landing.art.props.4.label": "Path to the chapel",
 "case:locations.landing.description": "The trust’s work boat puts you ashore at the Landing, a stone jetty in a sheltered cove on the island’s south-west side. Beside it stands the visitor hut: benches, a tea urn, maps and a sightings board. From here the path climbs past the chapel to the lighthouse and the north end. Down in the cove your phone shows no signal at all.",
 "case:locations.landing.hotspots.hs_ld_board.label": "The sightings board",
 "case:locations.landing.hotspots.hs_ld_board.text": "Sunday’s sightings are still on the whiteboard, in Sam’s capitals: ‘SIBERIAN RUBYTHROAT!!! (found Sat by S.O.) — foghorn gorse. LAST BOAT 16:30 — BE AT THE LANDING BY 16:15 PLEASE. SEAL HAVEN CLOSED FOR PUPPING — KEEP OUT.’",
 "case:locations.landing.hotspots.hs_ld_hut.label": "The visitor hut",
 "case:locations.landing.hotspots.hs_ld_hut.text": "Benches round the walls, a tea urn, a card machine and a rack of soggy leaflets. On Sunday afternoon it was full of people out of the wind, Tom among them, reading by the window. The card machine’s paper roll is still spiked by the till.",
 "case:locations.landing.hotspots.hs_ld_jetty.label": "The jetty",
 "case:locations.landing.hotspots.hs_ld_jetty.text": "Sunday’s queue stood here, along the jetty. From its foot you can see the last two hundred metres of the path coming down from the chapel, open turf all the way: nobody comes down that path without being seen, least of all in a yellow jacket. At the far end, steps go down into green water four fathoms deep.",
 "case:locations.landing.hotspots.hs_ld_router.label": "The Wi-Fi router",
 "case:locations.landing.hotspots.hs_ld_router.text": "On a shelf above the urn is a small white router with a card taped to it: ‘EbbaGuest — free Wi-Fi for visitors’. It’s the only connection there is down here in the cove; the card machine runs on it too.",
 "case:locations.landing.name": "The Landing",
 "case:locations.landing.summary": "St Ebba’s stone jetty, the visitor hut, and the foot of the path.",
 "case:locations.north.art.props.0.label": "Foghorn gorse",
 "case:locations.north.art.props.1.label": "Foghorn",
 "case:locations.north.art.props.2.label": "The Churn",
 "case:locations.north.art.props.3.label": "Lighthouse",
 "case:locations.north.art.props.4.label": "Oil store",
 "case:locations.north.art.props.5.label": "Cottage",
 "case:locations.north.art.props.6.label": "Cliff path",
 "case:locations.north.art.props.7.label": "Seal Haven (closed)",
 "case:locations.north.art.props.8.label": "To the chapel",
 "case:locations.north.description": "The north end is the island’s high point. A squat white lighthouse stands beside the old keepers’ cottage, and a disused foghorn station sits at the tip, in gorse and bramble where the rubythroat hid. Out on the north-east spur is the Churn. A cliff path runs south along the east side, above the fenced cove of Seal Haven, where the pups are.",
 "case:locations.north.hotspots.hs_nt_churn.label": "The Churn",
 "case:locations.north.hotspots.hs_nt_churn.text": "You walk out along the spur to the Churn.",
 "case:locations.north.hotspots.hs_nt_gallery.label": "The lighthouse gallery",
 "case:locations.north.hotspots.hs_nt_gallery.text": "A narrow iron gallery runs round the top of the lighthouse. From it you can see the whole north end: the gorse, the spur out to the Churn, the cottage, and Seal Haven below the cliffs, where the seals are counted.",
 "case:locations.north.hotspots.hs_nt_gorse.label": "The foghorn gorse",
 "case:locations.north.hotspots.hs_nt_gorse.text": "A tangle of gorse and bramble round the old foghorn station, flattened at the edges where forty people stood with telescopes all Sunday. From here a spur of turf runs out to the north-east, to the Churn.",
 "case:locations.north.hotspots.hs_nt_oil.label": "The oil store",
 "case:locations.north.hotspots.hs_nt_oil.text": "A stone room at the foot of the lighthouse, with an iron door, locked for the winter. The main path back from the Churn runs right past it. Sam has the other key.",
 "case:locations.north.hotspots.hs_nt_path.label": "The cliff path",
 "case:locations.north.hotspots.hs_nt_path.text": "The cliff path runs south from the lighthouse along the east side, above Seal Haven. From a few hundred metres along it you can look back and see the Churn wall on its headland, small and clear against the sky.",
 "case:locations.north.name": "The North End",
 "case:locations.north.summary": "The lighthouse and the keepers’ cottage, the foghorn gorse, the Churn, and the cliff path.",
 "case:setting": "St Ebba’s Isle · Northumberland · September 2025",
