 "case:timeline.events.tl_text.time": "16:22",
 "case:timeline.prompt": "Put the events in the order they really happened. Times you know for certain are shown; reason out the rest.",
 "case:timeline.title": "Sunday afternoon, reconstructed",
 "case:title": "The Last Ferry",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_gaz_chapel.insight": "Gaz never went to the chapel garden. From 16:05 to 16:14 he was photographing seal pups inside the fence of Seal Haven, closed since 1 September. On his way there, from the cliff path, he saw Nell at the Churn with somebody in dark clothes.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_gaz_chapel.response.0": "…Them pups. Aye. I was in the Haven.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_gaz_chapel.response.1": "When the bird went, I took the cliff path in case it dropped into the scrub along the top. It didn’t. And the pups were right there below me, and the fence is only waist high. I’ve a caution already, for the terns. They’d take every permit I’ve got.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_gaz_chapel.response.2": "Before I went over the fence I looked back up at the Churn. Two of them at the wall, close together: a yellow jacket and somebody in dark clothes. Too far to tell you man or woman. I thought: two birders falling out over a gull. And I ducked down so they’d not see me go over the fence.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_gaz_chapel.response.3": "Five minutes later, from inside the fence, I looked up again, and there was nobody at the wall. And nobody came down that cliff path past me. I’d have seen them against the sky.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_gaz_chapel.title": "Gaz was in Seal Haven",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_kirsty_count.insight": "Kirsty’s counter still reads 039. Thirty-nine people stepped aboard at the Landing, though forty tickets were scanned. She trusted the scanner, and then told the MCA that her counter agreed with it.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_kirsty_count.response.0": "…Thirty-nine. Aye. I know what it says.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_kirsty_count.response.1": "The birders came in a rush at the end, and I thought I’d missed one with my thumb. The scanner said forty, and the scanner’s never wrong, is it? So I told my dad forty, and he took the boat out.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_kirsty_count.response.2": "When the MCA asked, I said my counter agreed. If I’d said thirty-nine, it’s us that sailed knowing. That’s my dad’s licence. That’s sixty years.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_kirsty_count.title": "The counter said thirty-nine",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_morag_churn.insight": "Nell never walked away from the Churn. Nobody came down the cliff path past Gaz, and what she had with her went back the other way, past the lighthouse. Morag pushed her over the wall, then took her phone down to the Landing and answered Tom.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_morag_churn.response.0": "Morag looks at what you’ve brought her for a long time.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_morag_churn.response.1": "I taught her to ring her first puffin. Did you know that? On that very wall. She cried when it bit her.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_morag_churn.response.2": "She said, ‘You’ve had twenty-four wonderful years, Morag.’ As if I’d been on holiday. As if it had all been lent me. I put my hands on her shoulders, and she went over. The Churn did the rest. It always does.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_morag_churn.response.3": "Then Rob came on the radio, cheerful as you like, and I answered him. The rest was easy. Her phone was lying on the wall, and down at the Landing Tom kept asking everybody where she was. I only had to answer him.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_morag_churn.response.4": "She doesn’t say anything else.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_morag_churn.title": "Nell never left the Churn",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_morag_lighthouse.insight": "Morag wasn’t indoors. At four, when Rob Dunn radioed from the Landing, she answered over the wind with the Churn booming behind her. She says Nell had asked to talk, and that they met at the Churn just before four, and that Nell then walked off down the cliff path, alive.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_morag_lighthouse.response.0": "…The Churn. Aye. You can’t mistake it, can you, once you’ve heard it.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_morag_lighthouse.response.1": "Nell asked to talk. She sent me a message from the foghorn: she’d rather I heard it from her than from the board. I said the Churn, at four, where nobody would hear us. She told me what her plan would do to this island, and to me. Twenty-four years.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_morag_lighthouse.response.2": "Then she walked off down the cliff path towards the chapel, alive. That’s the last I saw of her. I didn’t tell the police because they never asked, and because I knew how it would look.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_morag_lighthouse.title": "Morag was out at the Churn",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_sam_sweep.insight": "At 16:00:48 Sam was in the chapel garden, behind the rubythroat in J. Purvis’s photo. When the bird flew he ran after it and skipped the sweep, and he radioed ‘north end clear’ from the chapel garden.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_sam_sweep.response.0": "…That’s me. Behind the bird, with my camera up.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_sam_sweep.response.1": "When it flew at 15:52, I knew where it’d go: the chapel elders, they always come back to them. So I ran. I never did the sweep. I radioed ‘north end clear’ from the chapel garden, so Morag wouldn’t know.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_sam_sweep.response.2": "I’ve thought about it every night since. If I’d done the sweep, I’d have been at the Churn at four. I’d have seen her.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_sam_sweep.title": "Sam never swept the north end",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_tom_row.insight": "At about ten past three Tom and Nell rowed in the chapel ruins. He had lost £38,000 of their savings on a trading app. On the boat he thought she was keeping away from him, so he never went looking for her.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_tom_row.response.0": "…Sam heard that. Of course he did. Half the island probably heard it.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_tom_row.response.1": "I’d lost thirty-eight thousand pounds of our savings on a trading app. Nell found out on Saturday night. At the chapel she said, ‘You promised me you’d stopped,’ I said I’d pay it back, and she walked off up the island.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_tom_row.response.2": "That’s why I never went looking for her on the boat. I thought she was keeping away from me. I sat by the door with her seat and thought: fine, Seahouses, then. And she was never even on it.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_tom_row.response.3": "And before you ask, there’s a life policy. Two hundred and fifty thousand. The police asked me about it on the first day.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_tom_row.title": "They rowed at the chapel",
 "deductions:deductions.d_churn.conclusion": "At 15:53, as the bird flew, Nell walked away from the crowd, alone, out along the spur to the Churn. At about two minutes to four Gaz saw her at the Churn wall with somebody in dark clothes. A few minutes later nobody was there, and nobody came back down the cliff path past him. The spur is a dead end: whoever was with her went back by the main path, past the lighthouse.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_churn.options.0": "In the chapel garden, with the crowd watching the bird",
 "deductions:deductions.d_churn.options.1": "On the path down to the Landing, at twenty past four",
 "deductions:deductions.d_churn.options.2": "At the Churn, just before four, with somebody in dark clothes",
 "deductions:deductions.d_churn.options.3": "In the visitor hut with Tom, at quarter past three",
 "deductions:deductions.d_churn.proofLabels.0": "Where she was going at 15:53",
 "deductions:deductions.d_churn.proofLabels.1": "Who saw her there",
 "deductions:deductions.d_churn.question": "Where was Nell last seen alive?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_churn.title": "Last seen at the Churn",
 "deductions:deductions.d_count.conclusion": "Forty tickets were scanned at the Landing, but only thirty-nine people stepped aboard: Kirsty’s counter still says so. Nell’s ticket went through on Tom’s phone, two seconds after his own. Thirty-nine people got on at the island, and thirty-nine got off at Seahouses. Nell never left St Ebba’s.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_count.options.0": "Forty: the scanner read forty tickets",
 "deductions:deductions.d_count.options.1": "Forty-one: a ranger came over without a ticket",
 "deductions:deductions.d_count.options.2": "Thirty-nine: Gaz Milburn’s ticket was scanned, but he missed the boat",
 "deductions:deductions.d_count.options.3": "Thirty-nine: Nell’s ticket was scanned on Tom’s phone, but she never got on",
 "deductions:deductions.d_count.proofLabels.0": "Whose phone Nell’s ticket was on",
 "deductions:deductions.d_count.proofLabels.1": "How many people stepped aboard",
 "deductions:deductions.d_count.question": "How many people really got on the Ebba Belle at the Landing?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_count.title": "Forty tickets, thirty-nine people",
 "deductions:deductions.d_deck.conclusion": "Six people were on the upper deck for the loop, all men with telescopes, and none of them saw a woman. For fifteen years Nell sat inside by the cabin door on every crossing, and on Sunday she asked Tom to save her a seat inside. If she was on the Ebba Belle at all, she was never on the upper deck.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_deck.options.0": "Yes: everyone goes up for the seals on the loop",
 "deductions:deductions.d_deck.options.1": "Yes: she went up to be sick over the rail",
 "deductions:deductions.d_deck.options.2": "No: nobody up there saw her, and she always sat inside",
 "deductions:deductions.d_deck.options.3": "No: the upper deck was closed in the swell",
 "deductions:deductions.d_deck.proofLabels.0": "Who was on the upper deck",
 "deductions:deductions.d_deck.proofLabels.1": "Where Nell always sat",
 "deductions:deductions.d_deck.question": "Did Nell go up to the upper deck on the crossing?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_deck.title": "Never on the upper deck",
 "deductions:deductions.d_motive.conclusion": "Nell’s plan would take the rangers off St Ebba’s, turn the keepers’ cottage into a holiday let, and end the day trips. It was going to the board on Tuesday. The staff it hurt were to be told in person before Tuesday, and on Friday the trust told Morag that Nell would be over on Sunday and wanted a word. Nell came over on the last landing day to tell the island’s rangers herself.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_motive.options.0": "To see the Siberian rubythroat before it moved on",
 "deductions:deductions.d_motive.options.1": "To warn the Dunns that their day trips would end",
 "deductions:deductions.d_motive.options.2": "To tell the rangers herself that the trust meant to replace them",
 "deductions:deductions.d_motive.options.3": "To make it up with Tom, away from home",
 "deductions:deductions.d_motive.proofLabels.0": "What Nell’s plan would do",
 "deductions:deductions.d_motive.proofLabels.1": "Whom she came to tell",
 "deductions:deductions.d_motive.question": "Why did Nell come to St Ebba’s on the last landing day?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_motive.title": "Something to say face to face",
 "deductions:deductions.d_text.conclusion": "At 16:22 Nell’s phone was on the visitor hut’s Wi-Fi, the only connection at the Landing, for less than a minute and a half. Nell wasn’t there: Tom was watching the path for her, she never got on the boat, and the message isn’t even written the way she wrote. Somebody standing in the crowd at the Landing had her phone in a pocket, read Tom’s messages, and answered him.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_text.options.0": "Nell, on her way down from the chapel",
 "deductions:deductions.d_text.options.1": "Nell, from the north end, while she still had a signal",
 "deductions:deductions.d_text.options.2": "Nell, from the boat, after she had slipped aboard",
 "deductions:deductions.d_text.options.3": "Somebody at the Landing, with Nell’s phone in their pocket",
 "deductions:deductions.d_text.proofLabels.0": "Where Nell’s phone was at 16:22",
 "deductions:deductions.d_text.proofLabels.1": "Whether Nell sent it",
 "deductions:deductions.d_text.question": "Who sent Tom ‘Get on & save me a seat inside’ at 16:22?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_text.title": "Not her message",
 "deductions:deductions.d_who.conclusion": "Whoever was with Nell at the Churn just before four went back past the lighthouse, and answered Tom from the Landing at 16:22. Tom was paying for two teas in the hut at 15:57, sixteen minutes from the Churn. Sam was in the chapel garden at 16:00:48. Kirsty came in with the boat at 16:00. Gaz didn’t reach the Landing until 16:26, four minutes after the message. Only Morag was alone at the north end, three minutes from the Churn, and at the Landing in time: her phone joined the hut’s Wi-Fi at 16:18.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_who.options.0": "Tom Ashby",
 "deductions:deductions.d_who.options.1": "Gaz Milburn",
 "deductions:deductions.d_who.options.2": "Sam Okafor",
 "deductions:deductions.d_who.options.3": "Morag Heslop",
 "deductions:deductions.d_who.proofLabels.0": "Who was at the Landing when Tom got the message",
 "deductions:deductions.d_who.proofLabels.1": "Where the two rangers really were at four",
 "deductions:deductions.d_who.question": "Who could have been at the Churn just before four, and at the Landing at 16:22?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_who.title": "At the Churn, then at the Landing",
 "dialogues:statements.st_gaz_chapel.text": "When the bird flew at 15:52, I went straight down to the chapel garden with everybody else, and stayed there until I ran for the boat.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_gaz_churn.text": "At about two minutes to four, from the cliff path, I saw two people at the Churn wall: a yellow jacket and somebody in dark clothes. When I looked up again from inside the fence, five minutes later, nobody was there, and nobody came down the cliff path past me.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_gaz_deck.text": "I was on the upper deck for the loop with five other lads. There was no woman up there.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_kirsty_39.text": "My counter said thirty-nine. The scanner said forty, so I told my dad forty.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_kirsty_deck.text": "On the loop I had six on the upper deck: Gaz Milburn and five lads with telescopes. No woman.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_kirsty_forty.text": "I counted forty aboard on my counter, same as the scanner.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_kirsty_radio.text": "At four, when my dad radioed that we were alongside, Morag answered with the wind roaring and something booming behind her, like a big door slamming.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_kirsty_tom.text": "Tom Ashby scanned two tickets on his phone and said his wife was just coming.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_morag_churn.text": "Nell asked to talk, and I met her at the Churn just before four. She told me what her plan would do. Then she walked off down the cliff path towards the chapel, alive.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_morag_confession.text": "I met Nell at the Churn just before four, and I pushed her over the wall. Then I answered Tom from her phone.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_morag_lighthouse.text": "From twenty to four until five past four I was indoors, in the cottage and the lighthouse, and never set foot outside.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_sam_chapel.text": "When the bird flew at 15:52, I ran down to the chapel garden after it. I never swept the north end: I radioed ‘clear’ from the chapel garden.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_sam_email.text": "On Friday the trust emailed Morag that Nell would be on the island on Sunday and wanted a word with her about the review.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_sam_row.text": "At about ten past three I heard Nell and Tom rowing in the chapel ruins. She said, ‘You promised me you’d stopped.’",
 "dialogues:statements.st_sam_sweep.text": "At quarter to four I swept the north end: the foghorn, the Churn, the lighthouse. Nobody was there. At 16:04 I radioed ‘north end clear’.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_tom_day.text": "It was a lovely day. Not a cross word between us all day.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_tom_hut.text": "From about quarter past three I sat in the visitor hut with my book until the boat came. Just before four I bought two teas, one for Nell.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_tom_inside.text": "Nell was sick on every boat. She always sat inside, by the cabin door.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_tom_path.text": "I watched the path from the jetty until she messaged. Then I scanned both our tickets, got on and kept her the seat by the door. I never saw her come aboard.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_tom_row.text": "We rowed in the chapel ruins at about ten past three: I’d lost £38,000 of our savings on a trading app. On the boat I thought she was keeping away from me, so I didn’t go looking.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_tom_style.text": "When the 16:22 message came, I thought it looked odd. I put it down to her rushing.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_tom_why.text": "Nell had been dreading Sunday. She said she had to tell somebody something to their face, and it would break their heart.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gaz_day.lines.0": "Mr Milburn, tell me about Sunday.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gaz_day.lines.1": "Siberian rubythroat. First for Northumberland. I was on the first boat with my flask, and I never left that gorse at the foghorn all day. It’s all on the news. Here, have a look.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gaz_day.lines.2": "When it flew at 15:52, I went straight down to the chapel garden with everybody else, and I was there till I ran for the boat.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gaz_day.prompt": "Tell me about Sunday.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gaz_deck.lines.0": "Top deck, for the loop. Me and five lads from Teesside with their telescopes. There’s seals at the north end, and you might get a skua.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gaz_deck.lines.1": "There was no woman up there. Not in yellow, not in anything. I’d have noticed: it was blowing a hoolie, and we were hanging on to the rail like monkeys.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gaz_deck.prompt": "Where were you on the crossing back?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gaz_nell.lines.0": "Everybody knew Nell Ashby. In 2023 she was warden on the tern colony down the coast, and she reported me to the police for getting too close. I got a caution. A caution, for a photo.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gaz_nell.lines.1": "And this year she wanted birders off St Ebba’s unless we paid for her guided trips. I said what I thought of that online, and I’ll not pretend I didn’t.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gaz_nell.lines.2": "I’m not sorry she can’t ban us now. That’s not the same as wanting her dead.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gaz_nell.prompt": "Did you know Nell Ashby?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gaz_photos.lines.0": "Bird photos? Aye, go on. Don’t go scrolling too far, mind.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gaz_photos.lines.1": "He hands you his camera.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_gaz_photos.prompt": "May I see your photos from Sunday?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kirsty_boarding.lines.0": "We came alongside at four. First scan about ten past. Folk trickled down, then the birders came in a rush at the end, all scopes and elbows. Gaz Milburn was nearly the last, purple in the face.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kirsty_boarding.lines.1": "Tom Ashby showed me two tickets on his phone and said his wife was just coming. Folk do that all the time. You scan what they show you.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kirsty_boarding.lines.2": "You can have the log. I’ll print it off the scanner for you.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kirsty_boarding.prompt": "Tell me about the boarding on Sunday.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kirsty_count.lines.0": "Kirsty, how do you count passengers aboard?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kirsty_count.lines.1": "Two ways, every boarding. I scan every ticket, and I click every head with my counter as they step aboard, same as my granda did. My dad counts them off at the other end.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kirsty_count.lines.2": "Sunday at the Landing: forty tickets, forty on my counter. Then at Seahouses my dad counted thirty-nine off. She went over on the loop. That’s all anyone can think.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kirsty_count.prompt": "How do you count passengers aboard?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kirsty_deck.lines.0": "I always keep a count up top: twelve’s the limit. On the loop I had six up there, Gaz Milburn and five lads with telescopes. Men, every one. I went up twice to tell them to hold on.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kirsty_deck.lines.1": "No woman. I’ve told the police that. They said she must have gone up after I came down.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kirsty_deck.prompt": "Who was on the upper deck on the way back?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kirsty_radio.lines.0": "Dad radioed the island, same as always: ‘Ebba Belle alongside at the Landing.’ Morag came back on her handset: ‘Thanks, Rob.’",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kirsty_radio.lines.1": "You could hardly hear her for the wind. And behind her there was this boom, twice, like a big door slamming. Dad said she must be out on the cliffs somewhere, counting something.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kirsty_radio.prompt": "What happened when you came alongside at four?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kirsty_trust.lines.0": "The end of us, near enough. Sixty per cent of what we take is St Ebba’s landings, and it says trust boats only from 2026. My granda started those trips in 1964.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kirsty_trust.lines.1": "So aye, I’d heard of Nell Ashby. I’d never met her. I couldn’t have told you she was on my boat.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kirsty_trust.prompt": "What would the trust’s plan mean for Dunn’s Boats?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_morag_day.lines.0": "Ms Heslop, where were you on Sunday afternoon?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_morag_day.lines.1": "Up at the north end, where I always am on the last day. I did the seal count from the lighthouse gallery at twenty to four. After that I was indoors, the day book in the cottage and the checks in the lighthouse, and I never set foot outside till I locked up.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_morag_day.lines.2": "At five past four I locked the lighthouse and the oil store for the winter, and came down to the Landing to see the boat off. It’s all in the day book.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_morag_day.prompt": "Where were you on Sunday afternoon?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_morag_island.lines.0": "Fourteen pups so far this year. Eight hundred pairs of puffins in May. A chapel that’s stood since twelve hundred and something, and a lighthouse that doesn’t need me any more.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_morag_island.lines.1": "I came for one summer in 2002 and never went home. I haven’t another home, if I’m honest: a caravan at Beadnell for the winters, and I count the days till April.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_morag_island.prompt": "Tell me about St Ebba’s.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_morag_nell.lines.0": "She was my volunteer in 2010. A good one: she’d sit in a hide for ten hours and thank you for it. She went up in the world after that.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_morag_nell.lines.1": "On Sunday she was at the foghorn with the birders all day. I’d not have known her from the gallery but for that yellow coat.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_morag_nell.prompt": "Did you know Nell Ashby?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_morag_review.lines.0": "Reviews come and go. I’ve seen off four. Now they want cameras on poles to count the seals. Let them get a camera to pull a pup out of a gully.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_morag_review.lines.1": "There’s always some clever soul in an office in Alnwick with a plan for this island. The island doesn’t read them.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_morag_review.prompt": "What do you make of the trust’s review?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_sam_ashbys.lines.0": "Nell, all day, at the foghorn with the birders. She was nice to me. She said she’d found her first rare bird on this island too.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_sam_ashbys.lines.1": "And the two of them at about ten past three, in the chapel ruins. I was in the garden next door, and they were going at it. She said, ‘You promised me you’d stopped.’ He kept saying he’d pay it back. Then she went off up the island on her own.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_sam_ashbys.prompt": "Did you see Nell and Tom on Sunday?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_sam_day.lines.0": "Sam, tell me about Sunday.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_sam_day.lines.1": "Mad. Forty people for my bird. I found it on Saturday morning in the foghorn gorse, a red throat like a cut, and by Sunday it was on every app in the country.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_sam_day.lines.2": "At quarter to four I did the last sweep, like always before the last boat. North end first: the foghorn, the Churn, the lighthouse. Nobody about. At 16:04 I radioed Morag, ‘north end clear’, and then went down to the Landing to help Kirsty.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_sam_day.prompt": "Tell me about Sunday.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_sam_morag.lines.0": "Twenty-four seasons. She knows every seal on the island by name. She’s trained half the rangers in the north of England. Nell Ashby was one of hers, in 2010.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_sam_morag.lines.1": "On Friday the trust emailed her: Nell Ashby would be on the island on Sunday and would like a word with her about the review. Morag read it twice, went out to the seals, and didn’t come in till it was dark.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_sam_morag.prompt": "Tell me about Morag.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_sam_review.lines.0": "The review. It came round on Friday. I printed it and pinned it up in the cottage, then took it down again on Saturday so I didn’t have to look at it while I made my tea.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_sam_review.lines.1": "Here. Everyone on the island’s read it.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_sam_review.prompt": "What is the trust planning for the island?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_sam_wifi.lines.0": "Only in the visitor hut. There’s no signal at all down in the Landing’s cove, so the trust put a router in for the card reader. Visitors use it to post their photos. I look after it.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_sam_wifi.lines.1": "It keeps a log of every phone that joins. Hang on, I’ll open Sunday’s on the laptop.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_sam_wifi.prompt": "Is there any Wi-Fi on the island?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_tom_boat.lines.0": "It came in at four. I stood on the jetty and watched the path down from the chapel: you can see the last couple of hundred yards of it from there. I sent her a couple of messages. Nothing.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_tom_boat.lines.1": "Then at 16:22: ‘Get on and save me a seat inside, just coming.’ I had both our tickets on my phone, so I scanned us both and told the girl my wife was just coming. I got on and put my coat on the seat by the door.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_tom_boat.lines.2": "I never saw her come aboard. It was heaving: birders everywhere, telescopes, rucksacks. I thought she must have come on in the last rush.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_tom_boat.lines.3": "I remember thinking that message looked odd. I couldn’t have told you why. I put it down to her rushing.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_tom_boat.prompt": "What happened when the boat came?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_tom_day.lines.0": "Mr Ashby, tell me about Sunday.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_tom_day.lines.1": "It was a lovely day. A grim crossing, but it always is for Nell. She had her bird — she’d wanted that bird since she was a kid — and I had a book and a flask. Not a cross word between us all day.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_tom_day.lines.2": "From about quarter past three I sat in the visitor hut, out of the wind, with my book, until the boat came. She’d said she’d be back by four. Just before four I bought two teas, one for her, for when she came down.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_tom_day.prompt": "Tell me about Sunday.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_tom_nell.lines.0": "She spent her first summer on St Ebba’s as a volunteer, at twenty-four, ringing puffins, and she never got over it. This year she ended up running all the trust’s reserves.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_tom_nell.lines.1": "And she was sick on every boat she ever got on. She sat inside by the cabin door, with her eyes on the horizon, every single time. She’d no more climb that ladder to the top deck than fly.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_tom_nell.prompt": "Tell me about Nell.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_tom_why.lines.0": "For the bird, she said. But she’d been dreading Sunday all week. Something to do with the trust’s plans for the island; she didn’t tell me much, it was confidential.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_tom_why.lines.1": "On Friday night she said, ‘I’ve got to tell somebody something to their face on Sunday, Tom, and it’s going to break their heart.’",
 "dialogues:topics.t_tom_why.prompt": "Why did Nell go over on Sunday?",
 "evidence:evidence.bea_photo.detail": "A photo Bea gave you: Nell, aged 24, on the Ebba Belle in July 2010, on her way to her first summer as a volunteer on St Ebba’s. She’s on the bench just inside the cabin door, grey-green in the face, with a paper bag in one hand and a thumbs-up for the camera in the other.\n\nOn the back, in Nell’s writing: ‘Never again!! (Said every time.)’\n\n‘She sat there on every crossing for fifteen years,’ Bea says. ‘Inside, by the door, with her eyes on the horizon.’",
 "evidence:evidence.bea_photo.name": "Bea’s photo",
 "evidence:evidence.bea_photo.summary": "Nell in 2010, on the bench inside the Ebba Belle’s cabin door, green in the face.",
 "evidence:evidence.binoculars.detail": "Sam unlocks the oil store at the foot of the lighthouse for you: a cold stone room of shelves, rope and old paint. Behind a stack of paint tins on the top shelf is a pair of green binoculars on a yellow neck strap.\n\nA label on the strap, in neat capitals: ‘N. ASHBY — IF FOUND PLEASE RING 07700 900418’. The eyecups are crusted with dried salt, as if the spray had reached them.\n\nIn Gaz’s photo at 15:53 they were on Nell’s chest. The day book says the oil store was locked at 16:05 on Sunday, and Sam says nobody has opened it since.",
 "evidence:evidence.binoculars.name": "Nell’s binoculars",
 "evidence:evidence.binoculars.summary": "Hidden behind paint tins in the lighthouse oil store, which was locked at 16:05 on Sunday.",
 "evidence:evidence.bird_news.detail": "Gaz shows you the bird news app on his phone: the posts for St Ebba’s Isle on Sunday 28 September.\n\n10:40 Siberian Rubythroat still present, gorse below the old foghorn. (G. Milburn)\n13:15 Still showing on and off, foghorn gorse. (G. Milburn)\n15:47 Showing well, foghorn gorse. [photo] (G. Milburn)\n15:52 Flew south over the lighthouse. Lost. (G. Milburn)\n16:01 Relocated in the chapel garden! Showing well in the elders. [photo] (J. Purvis)\n16:24 Still in the chapel garden as we leave for the boat. (J. Purvis)\n17:40 St Ebba’s: a passenger is missing from the last boat. If you saw anything, please contact the coastguard. (moderator)\n\nJ. Purvis’s photo, taken at 16:00:48, shows the rubythroat on an elder branch, its red throat blazing. Behind it, a little out of focus, stands the island’s young ranger in his green fleece, with his own camera to his eye.",
 "evidence:evidence.bird_news.name": "Bird news, Sunday",
 "evidence:evidence.bird_news.summary": "The rubythroat flew at 15:52, and turned up in the chapel garden at 16:01, with a photo.",
 "evidence:evidence.board_summary.detail": "Sam’s printout of an email from the Northumberland Seabird Trust to all staff, Friday 26 September.\n\n‘Review of island reserves — summary for staff.\nSt Ebba’s 2026, a proposal by Dr Nell Ashby, Head of Reserves, goes to the board on Tuesday 30 September:\n1. Remote cameras and drone surveys to replace the resident ranger posts on St Ebba’s, with a seasonal day warden.\n2. The keepers’ cottage to become a research base and holiday let.\n3. From 2026, landings by the trust’s own guided trips only. Day trips by private operators, and unguided birding, to end.\nWe know this will be difficult news for some colleagues. Staff affected will be told in person before Tuesday.’",
 "evidence:evidence.board_summary.name": "The trust’s review",
 "evidence:evidence.board_summary.summary": "‘St Ebba’s 2026’: cameras instead of resident rangers, the cottage a holiday let, and trust-only landings.",
 "evidence:evidence.churn_scene.detail": "At the island’s north-east tip a spur of turf runs out from the foghorn to a deep cleft in the cliffs. In any swell the sea surges into it with a boom like a slammed door, and the spray comes up over the top. A waist-high stone wall runs along the edge, with a sign: ‘DANGER — THE CHURN — KEEP BEHIND THE WALL’.\n\nOn the far side of the wall the turf is torn at the lip, as if something heavy went over. The police never came up here: they were looking at a boat.\n\nA fingerpost stands at the end of the spur: ‘Lighthouse 3 min · Chapel 12 min (cliff path) · Landing 16 min (main path)’. The spur is a dead end. The only ways back are the cliff path, south along the east side, and the main path past the lighthouse.",
 "evidence:evidence.churn_scene.name": "The Churn",
 "evidence:evidence.churn_scene.summary": "A cleft in the north-east cliffs where the sea booms; a low wall, a warning, and a fingerpost with walking times.",
 "evidence:evidence.clicker.detail": "In the wheelhouse drawer, on a lanyard: a chrome tally counter, the kind you click with your thumb as each person steps aboard. It was Kirsty’s grandfather’s, she says, and she sets it to zero at the start of every boarding.\n\nIts little window reads 039.\n\nNobody has touched it since Sunday’s last boat.",
 "evidence:evidence.clicker.name": "Kirsty’s counter",
 "evidence:evidence.clicker.summary": "Her thumb counter from Sunday’s last boat. It still reads 039.",
 "evidence:evidence.gaz_photos.detail": "Gaz’s camera, frame by frame, with the times it recorded.\n\n15:47 — The rubythroat in the gorse below the foghorn.\n15:52:58 to 15:53:04 — A burst of twelve frames as the bird flies off over the lighthouse; it’s a speck. In the last four, at the edge of the frame, a woman in a yellow jacket is walking away from the crowd along the spur that leads out to the Churn: alone, with binoculars on her chest.\n16:05 to 16:14 — Twenty-two frames of grey seal pups, white and new on the shingle of Seal Haven, from a few metres away. They were taken inside the fence, in the cove that has been closed to everyone since 1 September.",
 "evidence:evidence.gaz_photos.name": "Gaz’s photos",
 "evidence:evidence.gaz_photos.summary": "15:53: Nell walking alone towards the Churn. 16:05–16:14: seal pups, from inside the closed fence.",
 "evidence:evidence.island_log.detail": "The island’s day book, in the cottage: a thick ledger in which the rangers log every boat, every count and every radio call. Sunday 28 September 2025, the last landing day, in Morag’s hand:\n\n10:15 Ebba Belle in. 40 visitors landed. Siberian Rubythroat still in the foghorn gorse.\n15:40 Seal Haven, counted from the lighthouse gallery: 14 pups.\n15:45 S.O. (radio): starting the last sweep, north end first.\n16:00 R.D. (radio): Ebba Belle alongside at the Landing.\n16:04 S.O. (radio): north end clear.\n16:05 Lighthouse and oil store checked and locked for the winter. M.H.\n17:15 Coastguard: passenger missing from the Ebba Belle, Mrs N. Ashby. Shore search until dark: north end M.H., south end S.O. Nothing found.\n\nAnd on Monday, in Sam’s hand: ‘07:50 Lifeboat recovered a body from the sea below the Churn.’",
 "evidence:evidence.island_log.name": "The day book",
 "evidence:evidence.island_log.summary": "Morag counting seals from the lighthouse at 15:40, Sam’s ‘north end clear’ at 16:04, the oil store locked at 16:05.",
 "evidence:evidence.police_summary.detail": "Northumbria Police — summary for the family, via the coroner’s officer. A Maritime and Coastguard Agency investigation is ongoing.\n\nSunday 28 September 2025. Ebba Belle (Dunn’s Boats, Seahouses): the last public sailing of the season from St Ebba’s Isle.\n16:09–16:31 — Forty return e-tickets scanned at the island’s Landing, among them those of Mr T. ASHBY and Mrs N. ASHBY (39). The vessel left at 16:34 and made the skipper’s customary last-day loop around the north end of the island.\n17:08 — At Seahouses the skipper, Mr R. DUNN, counted thirty-nine passengers ashore. Mrs ASHBY could not be found. 17:12 — Coastguard alerted. Lifeboat and helicopter searched the route and the north end until dark.\nMonday 29 September, 07:50 — Mrs ASHBY’s body recovered by the lifeboat from the sea below the cliffs at the north end of the island.\n\nPost-mortem (initial): drowning. A wound above the right temple from a blow against a hard edge, consistent with the vessel’s hull or rocks. Grazes to both palms. No alcohol or drugs.\n\nMrs ASHBY last communicated at 16:22, when she messaged her husband to board and save her a seat. Her ticket was scanned at 16:26. Her phone has not been recovered. She was wearing a yellow waterproof jacket. No passenger recalls seeing her on board. It is believed she went up to the open upper deck for the north-end loop, in a heavy swell, and fell overboard unseen. The upper-deck rail is 1.0 m high.\n\nNo suspicion of third-party involvement. The MCA is examining the operator’s procedure for counting persons on board, and the upper deck. Mr DUNN’s boatmaster’s licence is suspended pending the investigation. Inquest opened and adjourned.",
 "evidence:evidence.police_summary.name": "Police summary",
 "evidence:evidence.police_summary.summary": "Forty tickets scanned, thirty-nine people ashore, and a fall from the upper deck.",
 "evidence:evidence.router_log.detail": "The visitor hut’s Wi-Fi, ‘EbbaGuest’, is the only connection there is at the Landing: the cove gets no mobile signal at all. Sam opens the router’s log for Sunday afternoon on the trust laptop. Each device, with the times it joined and left:\n\nTom’s phone — 15:18 → 16:36\nKirsty’s phone — 16:01 → 16:35\nDunn’s scanner — 16:01 → 16:35\nSam’s phone — 16:12 → 17:21\nMorag’s phone — 16:18 → 17:20\nNell’s phone — 16:21:40 → 16:23:05\nGaz’s phone — 16:26:20 → 16:35\n…and thirty other phones, joining between 15:31 and 16:31 and leaving at 16:35 or 16:36.\n\nEarlier that day the log shows Nell’s phone once more, 10:16 → 10:31, when the morning boat came in. The router reaches about fifty metres: the hut, the jetty and the foot of the path.",
 "evidence:evidence.router_log.name": "The Wi-Fi log",
 "evidence:evidence.router_log.summary": "Every phone on the visitor hut’s Wi-Fi on Sunday afternoon. Nell’s was on it from 16:21:40 to 16:23:05.",
 "evidence:evidence.scanner_log.detail": "Dunn’s Boats — return tickets scanned at St Ebba’s Landing, Sunday 28 September 2025. Kirsty prints it for you from the scanner.\n\n40 of 40 tickets scanned · first 16:09:12 · last 16:31:02\n\n(extract)\n16:26:41 ASHBY T. · booking EB-2291 · ticket 1 of 2\n16:26:43 ASHBY N. · booking EB-2291 · ticket 2 of 2\n16:27:30 MILBURN G. · booking EB-2317 · ticket 1 of 1\n16:31:02 PURVIS J. · booking EB-2302 · ticket 2 of 2\n\nBoth Ashby tickets were on Tom’s phone; two seconds is the time it takes to swipe to the next ticket. The scanner reads the code on a phone’s screen. It doesn’t count the people holding the phone.",
 "evidence:evidence.scanner_log.name": "The scanner log",
 "evidence:evidence.scanner_log.summary": "Forty of forty return tickets, 16:09–16:31. Both Ashby tickets went through two seconds apart, on Tom’s phone.",
 "evidence:evidence.till_roll.detail": "The visitor hut’s card machine prints a paper roll of every sale. Sunday afternoon’s, torn off and spiked by the till:\n\n14:41 · 1 × tea, 1 × flapjack · card ending 3301\n15:22 · 1 × tea · card ending 0915\n15:57 · 2 × tea, 1 × flapjack · card ending 6620 (T. ASHBY)\n16:13 · 1 × tea · card ending 2208\n\nThe hut is at the Landing, sixteen minutes’ walk from the Churn by the main path.",
 "evidence:evidence.till_roll.name": "The hut’s till roll",
 "evidence:evidence.till_roll.summary": "The card machine’s sales on Sunday afternoon. 15:57: two teas and a flapjack, on Tom Ashby’s card.",
 "evidence:evidence.tom_messages.detail": "Screenshots of Tom’s phone, which Bea forwarded to you: his messages with Nell on Sunday 28 September.\n\n10:16 Nell: made it, sea was grim 🤢 never again x\n10:52 Nell: its showing!!! gorse by the foghorn, come up\n11:05 Tom: On my way 🐦\n13:40 Nell: going back up for the afternoon, back by 4 x\n14:58 Tom: Chapel at 3? Need to talk x\n15:02 Nell: ok\n16:14 Tom: Boat’s here. Where are you? x\n16:19 Tom: They’re boarding!!\n16:22 Nell: Get on & save me a seat inside. Just coming x\n16:40 Tom: Where are you sitting? I’m by the door x\n16:51 Tom: Nell?\n17:09 Tom: NELL",
 "evidence:evidence.tom_messages.name": "Tom’s messages",
 "evidence:evidence.tom_messages.summary": "Sunday’s messages between Tom and Nell, ending with hers at 16:22: ‘save me a seat inside’.",
 "evidence:evidence.upper_deck.detail": "The Ebba Belle’s upper deck is open to the sky above the cabin: four slatted benches, and a white rail 1.0 metres high round the edge. A police seal is still taped to one of the stanchions.\n\nYou reach it by an iron ladder from the open stern deck, almost vertical, its rungs worn smooth. A brass plate at the bottom: ‘UPPER DECK — MAX 12 PERSONS — USE BOTH HANDS ON THE LADDER’.\n\nFrom the stern end of the upper deck, a fall would go straight into the wake, out of sight of the wheelhouse. That’s where the police believe Nell went over.",
 "evidence:evidence.upper_deck.name": "The upper deck",
 "evidence:evidence.upper_deck.summary": "Open to the sky, up a ladder you need both hands for. A 1-metre rail; twelve people at most.",
 "suspects:suspects.gaz.bio": "A bus driver from Ashington, and one of the best-known birders in Britain: 571 species seen in Britain, and two hundred thousand people watching his videos. He came over on Sunday for the rubythroat. He and Nell Ashby had history.",
 "suspects:suspects.gaz.dismissal": "Not with you, mate. Next question.",
 "suspects:suspects.gaz.name": "Gaz Milburn",
 "suspects:suspects.gaz.rebuttal": "I didn’t like her, and I said so, online, in capital letters. I never touched her. I was on that top deck with five lads and their telescopes.",
 "suspects:suspects.gaz.role": "The twitcher",
 "suspects:suspects.kirsty.bio": "Rob Dunn’s daughter. She has worked on the Ebba Belle since she was fourteen, and she’ll be the next skipper, if there’s still a boat to skipper. She scanned the tickets onto Sunday’s last boat.",
 "suspects:suspects.kirsty.dismissal": "You’ve lost me there, pet.",
 "suspects:suspects.kirsty.name": "Kirsty Dunn",
 "suspects:suspects.kirsty.rebuttal": "I was on the boat or on the jetty all afternoon, with forty folk round me. I never even met her.",
 "suspects:suspects.kirsty.role": "The deckhand",
 "suspects:suspects.morag.bio": "The senior ranger on St Ebba’s for twenty-four seasons. From April to October she lives in the old keepers’ cottage by the lighthouse, and she knows every seal on the island by name. She saw Sunday’s last boat off from the jetty.",
 "suspects:suspects.morag.dismissal": "I don’t follow you.",
 "suspects:suspects.morag.name": "Morag Heslop",
 "suspects:suspects.morag.rebuttal": "Twenty-four years I’ve looked after this island and every soul who lands on it. I was at the lighthouse. Read the day book. You’ve got this wrong.",
 "suspects:suspects.morag.role": "The senior ranger",
 "suspects:suspects.sam.bio": "The island’s assistant ranger, in his second season, from Leeds. On Saturday morning he found the Siberian rubythroat, the bird that brought forty people over on Sunday. One day he hopes to be the island’s ranger himself.",
 "suspects:suspects.sam.dismissal": "Sorry, I’m not sure what you mean.",
 "suspects:suspects.sam.name": "Sam Okafor",
 "suspects:suspects.sam.rebuttal": "I’d never hurt anybody. I was looking at a bird. That’s the worst thing I did on Sunday, and I’ll have to live with it.",
 "suspects:suspects.sam.role": "The assistant ranger",
 "suspects:suspects.tom.bio": "Nell’s husband, a software developer who works from home in Alnmouth. He’s no birder: he came over on Sunday to keep her company. He scanned both their tickets onto the last boat, and kept her a seat by the door.",
 "suspects:suspects.tom.dismissal": "Sorry. I don’t see what that’s got to do with anything.",
 "suspects:suspects.tom.name": "Tom Ashby",
 "suspects:suspects.tom.rebuttal": "She was my wife. I sat by that door for thirty-four minutes keeping her a seat. Whatever you think of me, I never hurt her.",
 "suspects:suspects.tom.role": "The husband"
}
